A fuzz target for the readers three programs share.

`libncfg_client.a` is linked by `ncfg`, by the Qt client and by the
NetworkManager shim, and every one of them learns what the machine is doing by
calling a reader here: framing, then JSON, then a typed walk that builds C
structures with allocated strings and counted arrays. The JSON layer was
already fuzzed from the daemon's side; the typed walk had nothing.

    replies   twenty-one one-request readers, the first input byte choosing
              which. A reply is delivered by a listening unix socket this
              process owns -- see `replies.c` for why the bytes are queued
              before the reader is called, and why there is no fork.
    monitor   the event STREAM, which is the other shape: one connection, the
              reader called again and again, a partial line kept between
              calls. None of that code runs in `replies`. It is also the
              GUI's reader -- `gui/src/monitor_stream.cpp` is this loop with
              a `QSocketNotifier` where the `for` is -- and the loop is
              bounded, because a reader with three answers and a buffer it
              keeps can fail to progress without failing.

**What the GUI itself parses is not here, and that is a measurement rather
than an omission.** Its views split strings this library already parsed --
`settings.members.split(' ')`, `peer.allowed_ips.split(' ')` -- inside widget
classes that need a `QApplication`, so there is no parser to point a target at
and nothing a fuzzer could hand them that the library would have passed
through. What the window reads from netcfgd is these two targets.

`make_seeds.py` derives the corpus from `doc/schema/socket.json`, which is the
protocol's own record of every request and response and is what
`tests/client_test` validates against. Re-run it when the schema grows.

**A corpus is part of the detector.** Measured 2026-10-21: with the
schema-derived seeds alone, a deliberate off-by-one in `read_routes` survived
971,215 executions unnoticed, because nothing in the corpus reached that
function -- the reply that does is a document whose networks carry routes, and
the schema has no such line. One seed later the same sabotage was caught in
seconds, as a heap-buffer-overflow in `ncfg_saved_networks_free` with the line
number. `seed-document-saved-networks.bin` is that seed and
`routes-past-the-count.bin` is the reproducer it produced.

    make -C client fuzz-replay   the corpus, deterministic, in `make check`
                                 through `client-test`
    make fuzz                    this target, the shim's three and the
                                 daemon's five
